WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:06.840
Chaos had erupted, all hell had broken loose,
and nightmares were running amok.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:11.490
Even from her perspective, watching as fleshy
abominations tore through the streets of New

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:17.699
York City, Eliza Flynn couldn’t escape the
feeling that the entire world had gone mad.

00:00:17.699 --> 00:00:23.779
Only moments before, things had been calm,
normal, another mundane, uninteresting day

00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:25.080
in the Big Apple.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:31.029
Now, she was watching in horror from underneath
an overturned table as a group of… things

00:00:31.029 --> 00:00:34.000
had seemingly appeared out of nowhere.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:40.610
Unbeknownst to Eliza, these creatures were
instances of something known as SCP-610, alternatively

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:43.610
going by the name the Flesh that Hates.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:48.700
From her hiding place, inside the local coffee
shop she frequented, they looked like horrific,

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:54.160
mutated masses of flesh that barely resembled
anything even close to human.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.020
But that’s what they were… or at least,
had been.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:02.570
Eliza had witnessed a barista rushing out
into the street, trying to get away from a

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:07.510
group of SCP-610s that were bashing their
appendages against the front window.

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:12.150
In trying to make a break for it, the mutated
creatures attacked the barista, killing him

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.460
upon making contact with his skin.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:19.400
It was only a short while afterwards that
Eliza spotted his body moving, the infectious

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:24.760
process only accelerated by the heat from
a nearby car that was on fire.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:29.940
Before long, what had once been a barista,
an ordinary person, was now another of the

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.090
grotesque Flesh monsters.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:35.420
Mike, she remembered, his name had been Mike.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:39.240
She’d read it on his nametag when she came
in for her morning coffee.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:44.329
He had always smiled, never got the spelling
of her name right on her to-go cup, writing

00:01:44.329 --> 00:01:46.480
it with an ‘S’ instead of a ‘Z’.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:48.799
And now he was gone.

00:01:48.799 --> 00:01:55.079
Worse than just dead, he wasn’t even Mike
anymore, instead turned into another SCP-610

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:56.570
monstrosity.

00:01:56.570 --> 00:02:00.840
Too petrified to move, terrified of being
caught and turned into another of the fleshy

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.680
nightmares, Eliza stayed hidden.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:09.550
This was it, she thought to herself, any moment
now, one of those things would find her, either

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:13.790
breaking through the street-facing window
of the coffee shop, or slithering in through

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:15.490
the rear fire door.

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:19.200
Sure enough, the Flesh formerly known as Mike
the barista turned.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:24.381
Where had once been a head was now a featureless
blob that had fused with the shoulder, but

00:02:24.381 --> 00:02:29.940
Eliza could tell its eyeless stare was being
directed at her… and she had nowhere to

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:30.940
run.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:36.680
Mike’s arm, which was now a slimy, blood-red
tentacle, began slamming against the glass,

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:39.069
cracks forming across the surface.

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:43.800
Eliza’s eyes darted around the room, trying
to look for some kind of way out.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:49.080
Her options were limited, and even then, they
didn’t amount to more than hiding in a storage

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:52.129
cupboard and prolonging the inevitable.

00:02:52.129 --> 00:02:54.300
She needed a miracle.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:59.130
As if to answer her prayers, a blinding white
light erupted from somewhere out of view,

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:00.600
beyond the window.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:05.240
Turning away, Eliza had to shield her eyes
from the searing glow, blinking away spots

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:08.459
in her vision as she looked to see what had
happened.

00:03:08.459 --> 00:03:14.769
The SCP-610 creature – Mike – had vanished,
a dark outline scorched against the glass,

00:03:14.769 --> 00:03:18.280
as if it had been obliterated by an intense
focused blast.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:21.950
That light, whatever it was, had vaporized
it.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:26.280
The sound of commotion came bustling through
the door, and distorted, mechanical-sounding

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:29.300
voices calling out.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.340
“Hello?

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:33.140
Is anyone in here?”

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:34.140
one garbled.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:36.750
“We mean you no harm,” another said.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:39.800
“But you need to leave this place, as soon
as you can.”

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:43.040
Cautiously, Eliza stood up from under the
table.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:46.680
Standing at the entrance to the coffee shop
were a trio of figures.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:51.100
Each one of them had machinery, all manner
of metallic components protruding from their

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:55.129
bodies, replacing arms, legs, parts of their
faces.

00:03:55.129 --> 00:04:00.080
Eliza stared at them, unsure whether these
cyborgs were as harmless as they claimed;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.739
one even had a cannon in place of one of their
hands, which was still smoking from the blast

00:04:04.739 --> 00:04:08.080
that had killed the SCP-610 outside.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.180
“Did the Flesh make contact with you?”
one of the figures asked.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:13.290
“No,” Eliza replied.

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:15.129
“That’s how they spread, right?

00:04:15.129 --> 00:04:16.810
It’s like an infection…

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:18.190
I watched it happen.”

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:21.570
“Indeed,” the cyborg with the cannon arm
replied.

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:24.540
“The Flesh being loose is only the beginning.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.760
If you are uninfected, then we’d ask you
to come with us.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:29.240
We’ll explain on the way.”

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:30.810
“Hang on, who are you?”

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:33.039
Eliza asked, concern on her face.

00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:35.050
“And where are you taking me?”

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:38.440
“We’re the Church of the Broken God,”
one of them answered.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:41.080
“And we’re getting you out of New York.”

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:45.000
The idea of a person making their way out
of the City That Never Sleeps on foot would

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:50.340
be laughable to anyone that lived there, even
before it had become overrun with monsters.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:54.740
But the subway wasn’t exactly running on
time, and the roads were gridlocked with cars

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:58.020
that had either been destroyed or abandoned
by their owners.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:01.720
So, walking out of New York was the only option.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:06.850
The Church of the Broken God cyborgs explained
to Eliza as much as they knew about what had

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:07.850
happened.

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:12.590
They described an organization, a secret cabal
with power spanning the entire world called

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:14.290
the SCP Foundation.

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:19.289
Apparently, this Foundation had been responsible
for gathering and safeguarding the world from

00:05:19.289 --> 00:05:21.550
dangerous and unexplained phenomena.

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:24.210
“You mean like those Flesh things?”

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:25.210
she asked.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:28.090
“Exactly, they called those SCP-610.”

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:31.770
But recently, something had changed.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:37.580
The Foundation had turned its seemingly inexhaustible
resources to the goal of exterminating humanity.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:44.030
It was by their hands that instances of SCP-610
had been unleashed in major cities; not just

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:49.199
New York, but Delhi, Tokyo, London, and countless
others.

00:05:49.199 --> 00:05:53.449
Those were far from the only anomalies the
Foundation had deployed either, as they walked

00:05:53.449 --> 00:05:58.210
the cyborgs described how someone called ‘Mr
Deeds’ had been assassinated heads of state

00:05:58.210 --> 00:05:59.530
the world over.

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:04.690
They also spoke of a horrifying reptile that
was now running wild and slaughtering everything

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:05.690
in its path.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:08.340
“We at the Church had to act.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:12.840
Whatever the Foundation’s reasons for unleashing
this hell, we couldn’t sit by and allow

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:14.770
them to destroy everything.

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:20.050
So, we’ve aligned ourselves with the Global
Occult Coalition, to assist them in fighting

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:21.730
against the Flesh that Hates.

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:25.770
In exchange, we have vowed to help transport
survivors to safety.”

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:28.760
“But where can we possibly go that’s safe?”

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:29.780
Eliza interjected.

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:34.060
“If the Foundation have the reach you say
they do, and if they have all these…

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:37.810
horrible creatures at their disposal, then
what chance do we have at surviving.”

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:42.740
“Well, there is always our path,” one
of the Broken God’s followers replied.

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:47.000
“The path of Standardisation, becoming one
with machine.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:50.180
Our mechanized parts are safe from the Flesh’s
corruption.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:52.090
If you would consider joining us…”

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:56.870
Eliza took a quick glance at the metallic
components of her cyborg companions; they

00:06:56.870 --> 00:07:01.560
looked like they hurt as much as becoming
a SCP-610 monster would have.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:05.600
She politely declined, although it led to
the rest of their journey continuing in an

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:07.190
awkward silence.

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:11.849
Eventually, the Broken God cyborgs brought
Eliza to an extraction point where they left

00:07:11.849 --> 00:07:14.819
her in the care of the Global Occult Coalition.

00:07:14.819 --> 00:07:19.129
Boarding a convoy, her and a number of others
were ferried out of New York state by the

00:07:19.129 --> 00:07:20.250
GOC.

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:25.130
For the first time since this had all started,
Eliza started to think that maybe she was

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:27.009
heading to somewhere safe.

00:07:27.009 --> 00:07:31.729
That sliver of hope all came crashing down
the moment the convoy was attacked.

00:07:31.729 --> 00:07:33.660
Armed assailants opened fire on the trucks.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:38.629
They were quickly identified by Coalition
soldiers as members of the SCP Foundation’s

00:07:38.629 --> 00:07:43.570
Mobile Task Forces, special operations troops
sent out to pick off survivors.

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:49.900
In the midst of the firefight, gunfire erupting
all around her, Eliza had never felt so terrified.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:53.180
One of the GOC officers turned to her as they
crouched behind cover.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:56.590
“Listen, I should be telling you this, but
there’s rumors of a survivor settlement

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:58.430
not far from here!” he barked.

00:07:58.430 --> 00:07:59.820
“I’ll draw their fire.

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:00.820
You run.

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:04.050
Get as far away from here, fast as you can,
and don’t look back!”

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:08.780
Doing as he told, Eliza sprinted away from
the wreckage of the convoy, until her legs

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:11.080
refused to run any further.

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:16.610
She trudged alone through an empty town, abandoned;
it felt like a place out of a nightmare.

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:20.460
The world was decimated, virtually post-apocalyptic.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:24.860
The further she willed herself to walk, the
more she could feel herself giving up that

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:29.639
she would ever find the settlement, if it
even existed at all.

00:08:29.639 --> 00:08:34.430
Hope had abandoned her, and at her lowest
point, Eliza dug her heels in and dropped

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:39.640
to the ground, both exhausted and convinced
she’d been hiking towards nothing.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:44.540
The feeling of being moved caused Eliza to
stir, waking her up from a dream that had

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:47.230
been almost as bleak as her new reality.

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:51.670
Someone was carrying her on what felt like
a stretcher, she quickly realized.

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:55.740
Before she had a chance to ask who they were,
the pair of shadowy human shapes brought her

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:58.160
through the woods to what looked like a campsite.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:02.329
“Don’t be alarmed,” one of them said
in a voice that sounded so optimistic it felt

00:09:02.329 --> 00:09:08.200
almost alien; as if this person was from another
planet where things were far less horrible.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:13.079
Looking around, Eliza noticed the glow of
a campfire illuminating the nearby trees.

00:09:13.079 --> 00:09:18.399
A handful of people were huddled together
in makeshift tents, resting and wearing contented,

00:09:18.399 --> 00:09:19.459
almost…

00:09:19.459 --> 00:09:20.459
happy expressions.

00:09:20.459 --> 00:09:26.579
Then, Eliza’s eyes were drawn towards something
else, some slimy mass gliding around the camp.

00:09:26.579 --> 00:09:32.560
At first, she thought it was another instance
of SCP-610, until she saw an amorphous, gelatinous

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:35.450
orange blob glowing in the firelight.

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:41.950
To the Foundation, it had been known as SCP-999,
but to its newfound family of human survivors,

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:47.710
this creature was their savior, a bastion
of hope and happiness in a devastated hellscape:

00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:49.380
the Tickle Monster.

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:55.600
Welcomed into the survivor community with
open arms, Eliza was soon exposed to SCP-999’s

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:56.760
anomalous effects.

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:01.459
The creature seemed to warm to her instantly,
greeting her with the enthusiastic friendliness

00:10:01.459 --> 00:10:02.990
of a small dog.

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:07.640
Even after as little as a single interaction
with it, she felt her lack of hope and depressed

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:12.130
feelings towards the bleakness of the world
had almost entirely faded.

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:18.140
She felt happiness, pure, genuine happiness,
for the first time in recent memory.

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:23.779
Thanks to the Tickle Monster, Eliza’s entire
outlook had completely changed.

00:10:23.779 --> 00:10:27.579
The same was true for the other survivors
that had formed a settlement around the happy

00:10:27.579 --> 00:10:28.910
orange creature.

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:33.640
They had all come seeking an escape, not just
a physical place that was out of harm’s

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:38.570
way, but somewhere they could reject the harsh
reality of what the SCP Foundation had done

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:39.720
to the world.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:46.010
And SCP-999 had provided that for them all,
curing the displaced refugees of their woes,

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:48.550
keeping them in perpetually high spirits.

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:53.160
Most of them had reached such a point of contentment
that they’d forgotten their old lives before

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:57.339
the collapse, before the anomalies and the
Foundation.

00:10:57.339 --> 00:11:01.830
Although the Tickle Monster demanded nothing
of its newfound family, apart from the occasional

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:06.560
playful tickle wrestle, Eliza and a group
of the other survivors would venture back

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:09.940
to the nearby abandoned town just beyond the
woods.

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:13.830
Once there, they would scavenge for as much
food as they could find, keeping a close eye

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:18.410
out for any candy with which to feed SCP-999.

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:23.620
Even though they knew 999 was more than happy
to provide positivity to the survivors, each

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:27.769
of them felt it was the least they could do
for their favorite orange slime ball.

00:11:27.769 --> 00:11:32.510
There was such a continuous state of love
and happiness in their camp that the survivors

00:11:32.510 --> 00:11:37.500
were hardly phased when a Foundation soldier
inadvertently stumbled upon their settlement.

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:43.510
Before he could raise his weapon and fire,
SCP-999 slid between its friends and the soldier,

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:45.649
blocking the path of his bullets.

00:11:45.649 --> 00:11:50.339
Without any shred of malice or hostility,
the delightful blob leaped towards the attacking

00:11:50.339 --> 00:11:52.520
MTF operative and embraced him.

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:56.279
Instantly, the Foundation foot soldier’s
demeanor changed.

00:11:56.279 --> 00:12:00.990
Before he had been glassy-eyed, emotionless,
like something deep inside him had broken.

00:12:00.990 --> 00:12:07.779
But after their hug parted, the man was euphoric,
grinning in a state of elation for long afterwards.

00:12:07.779 --> 00:12:12.350
For a time, Eliza sat with the soldier – Sergeant
Callows – and listened to him explaining

00:12:12.350 --> 00:12:16.820
how he wanted to renounce the Foundation,
to stay with this community of survivors now

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:19.579
he knew SCP-999 was here.

00:12:19.579 --> 00:12:22.350
“Do you remember anything from before?”

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:23.889
she asked curiously.

00:12:23.889 --> 00:12:24.889
“Before?”

00:12:24.889 --> 00:12:25.889
Callows echoed.

00:12:25.889 --> 00:12:28.010
“You mean before the Foundation…

00:12:28.010 --> 00:12:29.500
did all this?”

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:34.690
Eliza nodded; remembering the outside world
was hazy now, like trying to recall the vague

00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:36.139
details of a dream.

00:12:36.139 --> 00:12:39.020
“Not much,” the former MTF trooper answered.

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:42.779
“They wanted us to… to kill everyone because
of IT.”

00:12:42.779 --> 00:12:46.070
“What, like the Stephen King book?”

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:47.810
“No,” Callows chuckled.

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:53.240
“They told us IT had latched itself on to
everyone, all human subconscious.

00:12:53.240 --> 00:12:56.839
IT had plans, something really, really awful.

00:12:56.839 --> 00:13:00.300
So, the Foundation’s plan was to wipe everyone
out.”

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:03.640
“Wouldn’t that include everyone who works
for the Foundation?”

00:13:03.640 --> 00:13:10.930
“No, they… altered us, made it so we couldn’t
feel pain anymore,” he paused, then began

00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:15.779
giggling uncontrollably, as if he’d just
understood the punchline to a joke.

00:13:15.779 --> 00:13:19.930
“In fact,” Callows said between fits of
laughter, “That’s probably how 999 was

00:13:19.930 --> 00:13:21.160
able to fix me.”

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:25.630
Next to him, Eliza couldn’t help but laugh
too, the humor was infectious.

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:28.139
“Because you couldn’t feel pain?”

00:13:28.139 --> 00:13:29.350
she giggled.

00:13:29.350 --> 00:13:30.350
“Yeah!”

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:32.769
Callows exploded, delighted tears rolling
down his face.

00:13:32.769 --> 00:13:37.579
“I lost the capacity for it, I was hollowed
out, and that would’ve made it easy for

00:13:37.579 --> 00:13:40.720
SCP-999 to fill that space with… this!”

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:46.339
He rolled over onto the floor, still chortling
unstoppably, and didn’t stop for hours.

00:13:46.339 --> 00:13:51.090
The Tickle Monster’s powers of positivity
had increased exponentially since it had found

00:13:51.090 --> 00:13:54.830
itself free from the Foundation facility it
had used to live at.

00:13:54.830 --> 00:13:59.440
Much like Callow had explained to Eliza, it
had allowed the loveable blob to correct the

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:04.190
alteration the Foundation had made to the
former MTF sergeant, allowing him to feel

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:08.640
euphoria in the place of his total absence
of all pain.

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:14.250
And now, thanks to the influence of SCP-999,
the threat posed by both the SCP Foundation

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:20.630
and whatever this ‘IT’ entity was, seemed
so far away, so irrelevant, and did little

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:24.310
to dampen the high spirits of the survivors
in the camp.

00:14:24.310 --> 00:14:30.220
But in their collective sense of bliss, Eliza,
SCP-999, and the other survivors became a

00:14:30.220 --> 00:14:33.760
little oblivious to the outside world and
its horrors.

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:38.731
Little did they know, there was still danger
lurking much closer than they realized, one

00:14:38.731 --> 00:14:43.370
that had spread from miles away, and would
very soon be at the settlement…

00:14:43.370 --> 00:14:47.000
It was Eliza and Callow that first discovered
what was lurking nearby.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:51.840
They had both been searching for food in the
ruins of the old town beyond the woods, which

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:56.070
felt more and more like a completely different
world the longer they spent in the camp with

00:14:56.070 --> 00:14:58.000
SCP-999.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:02.380
As Eliza rummaged through the storeroom of
an abandoned supermarket, she heard a scream

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:05.519
of agony coming from nearby: Callow.

00:15:05.519 --> 00:15:10.290
For the first time in months, panic, fear,
and adrenaline rocked her system.

00:15:10.290 --> 00:15:12.380
It almost made her feel sick.

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:17.380
She had been so overloaded with positive endorphins
that it had practically numbed her to all

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:18.680
other feelings.

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:23.060
But instinct kicked in, fight or flight hitting
her with the force of an ice-cold bucket of

00:15:23.060 --> 00:15:26.839
water, and she ran out into the store to look
for Callow.

00:15:26.839 --> 00:15:31.410
What she saw gave her a terrified knot in
her stomach, the kind she hadn’t felt since

00:15:31.410 --> 00:15:33.800
the day the nightmare had started.

00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:39.889
The Flesh that Hates, an instance of SCP-610,
was standing in the aisles of the empty supermarket.

00:15:39.889 --> 00:15:44.600
The sight of the grotesque, mutated form shuffling
towards her made Eliza freeze on the spot.

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:48.470
It took a further moment to realize that Callow
had long since stopped screaming.

00:15:48.470 --> 00:15:53.100
Eliza’s thoughts had picked up speed; for
the first time in months, she was processing

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:57.290
information without it being dulled by the
euphoria brought on by SCP-999.

00:15:57.290 --> 00:16:03.459
It made her realize that this SCP-610 had
either killed Callow and he’d soon be turning

00:16:03.459 --> 00:16:09.089
into one of the Flesh creatures… or this
was Callow, and he’d already changed.

00:16:09.089 --> 00:16:13.241
Thinking fast, Eliza sprinted through the
supermarket, hearing the snarls of the Flesh

00:16:13.241 --> 00:16:14.600
coming after her.

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:19.279
She passed a shopping cart, and in an instant,
grabbed its handlebar, turning the metal cage

00:16:19.279 --> 00:16:24.120
around and racing full force towards the SCP-610
instance chasing her.

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:28.259
The cart collided with the blood-red mass
of Flesh, the sheer force knocking the creature

00:16:28.259 --> 00:16:34.079
onto its back and giving Eliza a window of
opportunity to race out of the supermarket,

00:16:34.079 --> 00:16:36.940
watched by a gaggle of mutated creatures.

00:16:36.940 --> 00:16:40.569
Those of them that still had eyes followed
her movements as she ran back towards the

00:16:40.569 --> 00:16:42.300
woods, towards the camp.

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:46.149
And slowly, the Flesh that Hates began to
follow.

00:16:46.149 --> 00:16:50.880
From the moment that she returned to the settlement,
SCP-999 could immediately sense that Eliza

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:52.829
was in a state of panic.

00:16:52.829 --> 00:16:57.139
Instinctively, the creature glided up to her,
only seeking to comfort her and make her forget

00:16:57.139 --> 00:16:59.180
about whatever had troubled her.

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:00.220
“No, stop!”

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:04.720
she protested, as much as she wanted to not
worry about the impending danger.

00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:09.600
SCP-999 recoiled from her, a wounded expression
on its gooey orange face.

00:17:09.600 --> 00:17:12.679
“I’m sorry, it’s just we’re all in
serious trouble!”

00:17:12.679 --> 00:17:13.829
Eliza urged.

00:17:13.829 --> 00:17:18.319
The other survivors gathered around lazily,
each of them seeming upbeat despite her grave

00:17:18.319 --> 00:17:19.319
news.

00:17:19.319 --> 00:17:21.189
“The Flesh, SCP-610, they’re here.

00:17:21.189 --> 00:17:22.620
They found us!”

00:17:22.620 --> 00:17:23.620
she explained.

00:17:23.620 --> 00:17:26.280
“They killed Callow, and it won’t take
them long to find us!”

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:31.610
“They won’t come here,” one of the other
survivors replied, not argumentatively, but

00:17:31.610 --> 00:17:34.260
in a more uplifting and reassuring tone.

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:39.299
“None of the other anomalies have found
us in months,” another interjected, smiling.

00:17:39.299 --> 00:17:41.750
“Not even the Foundation can get us here.

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:42.750
We’re protected.

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:44.690
Tickle Monster keeps us all safe.”

00:17:44.690 --> 00:17:48.000
“But what if it can’t protect us from
the Flesh?”

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:51.830
Eliza desperately tried to warn her fellow
survivors, but they were in too much of a

00:17:51.830 --> 00:17:55.090
carefree state to fear the approach of the
Flesh that Hates.

00:17:55.090 --> 00:17:59.460
A number of them tried to convince her to
relax, spend some time with the Tickle Monster

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:01.480
so she wouldn’t feel as bad.

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:05.020
As appealing as that sounded, Eliza didn’t
want to let her guard down.

00:18:05.020 --> 00:18:10.419
She felt she owed SCP-999 for keeping her
and the others alive, for helping them forget

00:18:10.419 --> 00:18:13.260
the horrific world the Foundation had unleashed.

00:18:13.260 --> 00:18:16.820
And, even if she was the only one, she’d
protect it.

00:18:16.820 --> 00:18:21.710
Night had fallen, the sound of every snapping
twig felt like it could be one of the SCP-610s

00:18:21.710 --> 00:18:22.710
approaching.

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:27.500
The rest of the camp was fast asleep, save
for Eliza, wielding a flaming branch in one

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:29.390
hand to ward off the Flesh.

00:18:29.390 --> 00:18:34.630
Sure enough, through the shadows of the woods
and the low light of her torch, she saw shapes

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:36.549
begin to shuffle towards her.

00:18:36.549 --> 00:18:41.690
The slick, bloody creatures looked even worse
in the dark, shambling over to Eliza, backing

00:18:41.690 --> 00:18:44.159
away cautiously as she brandished her flame.

00:18:44.159 --> 00:18:49.230
But the Flesh that Hates was closing in, more
and more of them slithering out of from between

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:52.890
the trees, causing her to step back in the
direction of the camp.

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:56.480
Suddenly, an excitable orange mass swept passed
Eliza.

00:18:56.480 --> 00:19:00.929
Under the torchlight, she saw SCP-999 approaching
the fleshy mutations.

00:19:00.929 --> 00:19:01.929
“No!”

00:19:01.929 --> 00:19:06.070
she cried out, horrified at what they would
do to her camp’s protector.

00:19:06.070 --> 00:19:09.929
To her surprise, the Flesh stopped in their
tracks as the Tickle Monster wiggled up to

00:19:09.929 --> 00:19:10.929
them.

00:19:10.929 --> 00:19:14.820
They seemed to get closer, examining the orange
goop as it grinned at them.

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:19.760
Then, as if they had all lost their innate
hostility to anything they hadn’t yet infected,

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:22.890
the SCP-610 creatures turned and left.

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:27.049
Perhaps it was something within the slimy
substance the Tickle Monster was made of that

00:19:27.049 --> 00:19:29.220
repelled the Flesh that Hates.

00:19:29.220 --> 00:19:34.789
Or maybe it was being such a sheer force of
overwhelming good, and its increased power

00:19:34.789 --> 00:19:38.630
that allowed SCP-999 to pacify the monstrosities.

00:19:38.630 --> 00:19:42.210
Either way, Eliza breathed a sigh of relief.

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:46.540
The grinning orange blob turned to her and
smiled, leaping up into her arms and allowing

00:19:46.540 --> 00:19:52.150
her to carry it back to the camp, as SCP-999
turned her relief into happiness once again.

00:19:52.150 --> 00:19:57.150
It seems that, no matter how bleak the world
might be, even in a timeline as horrifying

00:19:57.150 --> 00:20:02.990
as that of SCP-5000, wherever the Tickle Monster
goes, there will always be the promise of

00:20:02.990 --> 00:20:08.500
positivity… and with it, a sliver of hope
from the friendliest anomaly of all.

00:20:08.500 --> 00:20:16.070
Now go and check out “SCP-999 THE TICKLE
MONSTER” for “WHAT IF SCP-999 WORE SCP-035?”

00:20:16.070 --> 00:20:18.020
for more orange, blobby adventures!

